<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/foundational/value-colour-composition">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Visual Arts. You need nothing from earlier lessons — no
    drawing skill is assumed, and none is required yet. What this lesson builds first is
    <em>vocabulary</em>: the small set of levers every image, from cave painting to cinema
    frame, is built from, and the names that let you talk about what an image is doing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="element or principle? file the vocabulary" widget="sort-buckets" config={vccElemSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File the terms and the two halves of design appear: line, texture, form and colour are
    physical parts, while balance, contrast, space and composition are arrangements of them.
    Notice the why: made-of and put-together can be practised separately, which is exactly
    what makes them teachable.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>run the squint test and explain why value — not colour — is the structure layer of an image;</li>
    <li>name the elements (value, line, shape, form, texture, colour) and the principles that organise them (composition, space, balance, contrast);</li>
    <li>separate hue, value and chroma, and use complements, temperature and limited palettes deliberately;</li>
    <li>describe the three engines of depth — linear perspective, aerial perspective, overlap and scale — and where each one breaks down;</li>
    <li>analyse a composition's sequence of looking: focal contrast, leading lines, balance, negative space, edges;</li>
    <li>use the working cycle — record from observation, reflect, refine, adapt — that converts studies into finished work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you look at an image — including your own — you will ask which
    lever is doing the work, and what happens if it changes. Seeing before making.
  </p>

  <h2>The three levers every image pulls</h2>
  <p>
    Squint at any masterpiece until it blurs. What remains is a grey-scale pattern — big
    light shapes and big dark shapes. If that pattern reads clearly across the room, the
    image works at any size, in any light, at the back of a gallery; if it does not, no
    amount of beautiful detail rescues it. That squint is the fastest lesson in art: the
    three biggest levers an artist holds are <strong>value</strong> (how light or dark),
    <strong>colour</strong> (which hue, how intense) and <strong>composition</strong> (where
    the viewer's eye is sent). Beginners chase colour; professionals chase value.
  </p>
  <p>
    The levers have names because they can be practised separately. The <strong>elements
    of design</strong> are the physical parts of an artwork — line, shape, form, value,
    texture, colour. The <strong>principles of design</strong> are the ways those parts are
    arranged — balance, contrast, space, composition. Design divides exactly that way:
    what the work is made of, and how it is put together. This lesson takes them in order,
    then finishes with the working methods that turn vocabulary into images.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="colour as a conversation, 350 years long — physics, perception and paint trading ideas" widget="timeline" config={colourTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the timeline alternates between science and studio —
    Newton's prism (physics), Goethe's response (perception), Chevreul's dye workshop
    (industry), Munsell and Itten (teaching systems), sRGB (screens). Each entry changed
    what painters <em>did</em>, not just what they knew. Keep it in mind for the colour
    section: when you learn complementary contrast, you are learning Chevreul's discovery
    as the Impressionists received it.
  </p>

  <h2>Value and tone: the structure layer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Value</strong> — the lightness or darkness of a mark, independent of its hue —
    is the strongest lever, because the human visual system reads light–dark structure
    before it reads anything else: edge detection, depth perception and object recognition
    all run on luminance. A strong image usually has a clear <strong>value plan</strong> —
    a small number of big light/dark shapes (the classic discipline: no more than four or
    five values at the thumbnail stage). The fastest drill is <strong>notan</strong>, the
    Japanese light–dark design study: compose with only black and white, no grey allowed,
    until the two-value pattern alone is satisfying.
  </p>
  <p>
    Value also carries <strong>form</strong> — the illusion of three dimensions on a flat
    surface. A sphere is nothing but a value gradient: highlight, light, halftone, core
    shadow, reflected light, cast shadow. The old masters' <strong>chiaroscuro</strong>
    (Italian for "light–dark") is value orchestration at full power: Caravaggio drowned his
    compositions in darkness and let a single light source carve the figures out of it —
    the darkness is not empty, it is the stage the light performs on. <em>Tone</em> is the
    word for value used continuously — the smooth gradations between black and white — and
    it is tone that models drapery, skin and weather.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="the sphere's value scale, in order" widget="order" config={vccSphereOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Assemble the sphere's gradient in order — highlight, light, halftone, core shadow, reflected
    light, cast shadow — and the illusion of volume on a flat surface stands six gradations
    deep. Notice the why: chiaroscuro is value orchestration at full power, Caravaggio
    letting a single light source carve figures out of drowned darkness.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> value is the layer the eye reads first, and a good value
    pattern is the skeleton every other decision hangs on. Now the parts that make the
    shapes.
  </p>

  <h2>Line, shape and form: the drawing vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Line</strong> is the most human of the elements — nothing in nature is drawn —
    and it does three jobs at once. It <em>describes edges</em>: a contour drawing follows
    the boundary where an object meets its background. It <em>directs movement</em>: a
    diagonal pulls the eye faster than a horizontal, and an <em>implied line</em> — an
    alignment of shapes, a gaze between two figures — moves the viewer without a single
    stroke. And it <em>expresses temperament</em>: a nervous broken line and a calm
    continuous line carry different feelings before any subject is named, which is why
    gesture drawing is taught as feeling, not copying. <strong>Crosshatching</strong> —
    intersecting lines that follow a form — is line doing value's job, one of drawing's
    great economies: a few directional marks persuade the eye to manufacture fur, stone or
    water from nothing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="line's three jobs, plus its side hustle" widget="match" config={vccLineMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Match line's jobs: the contour describes edges, an implied line — an alignment of shapes or
    a gaze between figures — directs movement, the nervous broken line versus the calm
    continuous one expresses temperament, and crosshatching does value's job. Notice the
    economy named in the why: a few directional marks persuade the eye to manufacture fur,
    stone or water from nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shape</strong> is two-dimensional — the flat silhouette — and shapes divide
    into <em>geometric</em> (circle, square: human, ordered) and <em>organic</em>
    (irregular, found in growth and weather). Shapes sit on a surface called the
    <strong>ground</strong>, and the figure–ground relationship between them is a
    compositional fact you can design: the classic goblet/two-faces illusion shows the eye
    must be able to read <em>either</em> reading without disorientation.
    <strong>Form</strong> is shape given volume: a circle becomes a sphere, a rectangle a
    box, and the trick that converts one to the other is value plus edges (soft edges
    recede, hard edges advance). <strong>Texture</strong> is the surface quality, real
    (paint thickness — <em>impasto</em>) or implied. A composition is largely an
    arrangement of shapes whose silhouettes must read at a glance — which is why drawing
    teachers say "design the shadow shapes, and the object takes care of itself".
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="geometric vs organic shape" widget="compare" config={vccShapeCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Drag between the two shape families: geometric — circle, square — is the human, ordered
    vocabulary of things built, while organic irregularity is the vocabulary of things
    grown. Notice the why: the figure–ground relationship is a designable fact — the eye
    must read either reading without disorientation — and form is shape given volume, soft
    edges receding, hard edges advancing.
  </p>

  <h2>Colour: hue, value, chroma — and how colours change each other</h2>
  <p>
    Colour is three decisions pretending to be one, which is the insight behind Munsell's
    <strong>colour tree</strong> (1905) and every modern system since: <strong>hue</strong>
    (which colour — red, teal, ochre), <strong>value</strong> (light or dark) and
    <strong>chroma</strong> or saturation (how vivid or grey). Naming all three
    independently is what makes colour teachable: "mustard" is yellow, mid-value,
    low-chroma, and knowing that lets you mix it deliberately instead of finding it by
    accident. It also answers a discouraging myth — colour sense is not a gift you either
    have or lack; it is three separate skills with names, drills and diagnostics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="colour is three decisions pretending to be one" widget="cloze" config={vccThreeCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Fill the three independent decisions and colour stops being one hard problem: the family
    name, the lightness and the vividness become separate axes — Munsell's contribution,
    teachable and separately mixable. Notice the myth this kills in the prose: colour sense
    is not a gift you either have or lack but three skills with names, drills and
    diagnostics.
  </p>
  <p>
    The relationships that do perceptual work: <strong>complementary pairs</strong>
    (opposite on the wheel — orange/blue, red/green) create maximum vibration side by side
    and neutralise each other when mixed; <strong>temperature</strong> — warm colours
    advance, cool recede — is the painter's depth cue, running the atmosphere in every
    landscape from Turner onward; and <strong>limited palettes</strong> (three or four
    tubes, mixed) produce harmony because every colour on the canvas shares the mixing
    DNA. The timeline's central experiment belongs to Michel Eugène Chevreul (1839),
    chemist at the Gobelins tapestry works, called in because dyes that looked right on
    the sample looked wrong in the tapestry: his discovery — <strong>simultaneous
    contrast</strong> — is that adjacent colours change each other (a grey next to red
    looks greenish), a property of perception, not pigment. The Impressionists painted by
    it: Monet's shadows are purple <em>because</em> the light is yellow, placing
    complements side by side so the eye does the mixing. Goethe's <em>Theory of
    Colours</em> (1810) had already insisted colour is how the eye and mind respond, not
    just wavelengths — the poet and the physicist were both partly right, and painting
    lives in the overlap.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="one line of these palette notes is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={vccPalSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Click the faulty palette note and read the correction: simultaneous contrast runs the other
    way — a grey next to red looks greenish, adjacent colours changing each other toward the
    complement, a property of perception rather than pigment. Notice the lines that hold:
    complements vibrate side by side and neutralise when mixed, and Monet's shadows are
    purple because the light is yellow.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> shadows are not "black versions of the object" and
  white is not the absence of colour. Shadows are cooler, more saturated and more colourful
  than beginners paint them (compare a photo), and in pigment <em>black plus colour</em> is
  usually dead — value is lowered with the colour's complement or with raw umber. The
  squint test exists for exactly this: if the value pattern works, almost any hue choices
  will survive; if it doesn't, no colour saves it.</p>

  <h2>Space, perspective and scale</h2>
  <p>
    The picture plane is flat; depth is manufactured, and the manufacturing has three
    engines. Before linear perspective existed, painters used three intuitive cues —
    <strong>height</strong> (higher on the surface reads as farther), <strong>scale</strong>
    (smaller reads as farther) and <strong>overlap</strong> (nearer objects occlude farther
    ones) — and most human image-making, including Japanese prints and Chinese landscapes,
    still gets depth from these alone. <strong>Linear perspective</strong> — codified in
    Florence (Brunelleschi's demonstrations in the 1420s, Alberti's <em>On Painting</em>
    1435) — is an invention, not a fact about vision: parallel lines converge on
    <strong>vanishing points</strong> on a <strong>horizon line</strong> (the set of all
    eye-level vanishing points), and the receding lines are called orthogonals. The system
    is so persuasive it becomes meaning: Leonardo put the vanishing point of <em>The Last
    Supper</em> directly behind the head of Christ, extending the viewer's line of sight
    into infinity at exactly the composition's focus.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="place the invention of linear perspective" widget="place" config={vccPerspPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Place the demonstrations on the 1300–1600 scale: Brunelleschi in the 1420s and Alberti's On
    Painting in 1435 — a Florentine codification later exploited by Leonardo, who set The
    Last Supper's vanishing point behind the head of Christ. Notice the dates: the system
    arrived at a specific time and place, deliberately designed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Aerial (atmospheric) perspective</strong> uses what the air does: distant forms
    lose contrast, cool toward blue and soften — Leonardo's smoky mountains are the
    textbook. And <strong>scale</strong> inside the frame is expressive in its own right:
    the viewer's sense of a figure's size comes entirely from context — a doorway, a tree,
    a horizon line. An oversized figure in a small room reads as psychological pressure;
    the tiny pilgrims in a vast Alpine landscape read as human smallness (Friedrich built
    whole careers on the ratio). Depth cues, in short, are not just how the picture shows
    space — they are levers the artist sets.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> depth is manufactured by three engines — linear perspective (an invention with a birthplace
    and a decade), aerial perspective (what the air does), and overlap with scale — and each
    one is expressive in its own right.
  </p>

  <h2>Composition: balance, space, and the sequence of looking</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Composition</strong> is the directing layer: contrast, edges and leading lines
    steer the eye in a deliberate order — the artist composes the <em>sequence of
    looking</em>, not just the picture. Its instruments: <strong>focal contrast</strong>
    (the area of greatest value or colour contrast is where the eye lands first — place
    your subject where you want attention spent); <strong>leading lines</strong> (roads,
    arms, gaze directions — the viewer enters where the strongest line begins);
    <strong>balance</strong> (symmetrical balance is formal and stable; asymmetrical
    balance — a small dark mass countering a large light one, like a scale with a coin
    against a feather — is where the liveliness lives); <strong>negative space</strong>
    (the shapes between things are compositional objects in their own right, and many
    drawing courses drill them with a plant-plus-geometric-shape exercise in which the
    <em>gaps</em> are what you draw); and <strong>edge control</strong> (sharp edges
    attract, soft edges release — masters sharpen one area and deliberately lose the rest).
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="the eye lands in the wrong place — turn a dial" widget="branch" config={vccEyeBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Walk the critique's dials: focal contrast moves the greatest value or colour contrast onto
    the subject, leading lines re-point the strongest line to start at the entrance, and
    edge control sharpens the subject while deliberately losing the rest. Notice the ending
    drills: balance still matters, and the negative space between things is a compositional
    object in its own right.
  </p>
  <p>
    The famous recipes — the rule of thirds, the golden ratio, Itten's colour contrasts —
    are <strong>training wheels, not physics</strong>. The rule of thirds divides the field
    into thirds and places interest near the intersections; the golden ratio hides a
    similar proportion in a rectangle that regrows itself forever. Both are useful starting
    grids, and both get broken by the greatest artists: Caravaggio centres and drowns;
    Hokusai flattens space entirely in <em>The Great Wave</em> and it may be the most
    composed image ever printed. The rule exists to be <em>broken knowingly</em>: first
    make the image that follows the principle, then decide what breaking it buys you.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "composition rules are laws the masters obeyed." The
  rules were mostly written <em>after</em> the masterpieces, as descriptions of what had
  worked — which is why every one of them has a canonical violation. What the masters
  actually obeyed is the underlying principle the recipe approximates: a clear sequence of
  looking, shaped by contrast and edges. Learn the recipe to see the principle; then treat
  the recipe as a default you may override, not a law you must satisfy.</p>

  <h2>Recording and refining: the working cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Vocabulary becomes skill through two working methods the syllabus names explicitly.
    <strong>Recording from first-hand and secondary resources:</strong> first-hand means
    drawing from life — the still object on the table, the figure, the street — because
    observation teaches what no photograph pre-solves (photos flatten, auto-expose and
    already "chose" the values); secondary means reference — museums, photos, other
    artists — used deliberately for what you cannot access. The <strong>sketchbook</strong>
    is the instrument of both: quick observational studies, colour notes ("shadow on snow:
    blue-violet, not grey"), copies of masterworks copied <em>analytically</em> (why did
    Vermeer put the light there?), and thumbnails.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reflecting, refining and adapting</strong> is the cycle that converts studies
    into work: step back (literally — across the room, or in a mirror, or upside down, all
    of which defeat the eye's local adaptation), compare the image against the intention,
    diagnose which lever failed (value? composition? the colour temperature of the
    shadow?), and revise. Every professional painter works this loop — thumbnails before
    commitment, a value study before colour, colour studies before the final — because the
    loop is cheap and the final canvas is not. The habit is the skill: an artist is someone
    who has run the reflect–refine cycle enough thousands of times that the first guesses
    are already good.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "photographs replaced drawing from life." A camera
  makes the value decisions — exposure, flattening, focus — before you ever see the print,
  so copying a photo teaches you to copy, not to see. Drawing from life forces the
  decisions through your own hand, which is exactly why art schools survived the camera:
  observation is not a workaround for lacking a photograph; it is the training the
  photograph cannot give.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Your drawing's subject is hard to find at a glance. Name the first lever to check, and the diagnostic to run.</li>
    <li>"Mustard" — which hue, value and chroma? Why does splitting one colour name into three decisions matter?</li>
    <li>A friend says linear perspective is "just how eyes work". What would you show them to argue otherwise?</li>
    <li>A well-balanced painting feels unsettled rather than stable. Which kind of balance is likely doing this, and why would an artist want it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Value composition. Squint or posterise the image to
    four levels: if the big light/dark shapes do not isolate the subject, the problem is
    the value plan — not detail, not colour. (2) Yellow; mid-value; low chroma. Splitting
    the name matters because each axis is separately mixable and separately fixable — a
    wrong mustard is either the wrong hue, the wrong darkness or the wrong vividness, and
    only one of the three is usually at fault. (3) Any of: the pre-Renaissance images that
    show depth with height, scale and overlap alone; the Japanese prints that flatly reject
    it while still reading as deep; the fact that equal sizes shrink and parallel lines
    converge only because the system <em>declares</em> they must — a Florentine invention
    of the 1420s, not a law of vision. (4) Asymmetrical balance — a small, dark or complex
    mass counterweighting a large, light or quiet one. Artists choose it deliberately:
    perfect symmetry reads as still and formal; tension held in balance reads as alive.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Value is the structure layer: the squint test and the four-value thumbnail come before any colour decision.</li>
    <li>Elements are what a work is made of (line, shape, form, value, texture, colour); principles are how they are arranged (balance, contrast, space, composition).</li>
    <li>Colour is three decisions — hue, value, chroma — and adjacent colours change each other (simultaneous contrast), so palette is a relationship, not a list.</li>
    <li>Depth is manufactured by three engines: linear perspective (invented, not innate), aerial perspective (what air does), and overlap plus scale.</li>
    <li>Composition directs the sequence of looking — focal contrast, leading lines, balance, negative space, edges.</li>
    <li>Rules of thirds and golden ratio are training wheels for the underlying principle; break them knowingly, not accidentally.</li>
    <li>Recording from life, then reflecting and refining, is the cycle that turns vocabulary into skill.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Each lever here grows into its own lesson: line, shape and value deepen into
    observation drawing; colour theory expands hue, value and chroma into mixing systems
    and colour relationships; perspective and depth turns the depth cues into constructed
    space. The working cycle — record, reflect, refine — is the method every later lesson
    assumes, and the visual vocabulary you now hold is the language all of them are taught
    in. Next stop: drawing from observation, with a pencil and something real on the table.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A few layers for the committed. <strong>Colour constancy is the brain's edit:</strong>
    the visual system re-balances the whole scene toward its assumed light source, which is
    why a white page looks white indoors and out, and why painted colour must be judged in
    the painting's own imagined light — a snow shadow is painted blue-violet and
    <em>seen</em> as white. <strong>Gamut mapping and limited palettes are the same
    idea</strong> across media: choosing the subset of colours the piece will live in
    (Zorn's palette; a film's LUT) buys harmony at the price of range, and the sRGB gamut
    question — which colours does this display even have? — is gamut choice applied to your
    audience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Value grouping is the master's diagnostic:</strong> posterise any admired image
    to four levels and count the big shapes; the count is usually small, which is the
    actual secret the squint test measures. <strong>And the elements are culturally
    inflected, not universal:</strong> Western art history's perspective and modelling is
    one spatial grammar among several — Japanese ukiyo-e flat planes and expressive line,
    Islamic art's geometric pattern and calligraphic line, Chinese landscape's shifting
    perspective — which is why the timeline above is a Western conversation worth having
    and worth leaving: the levers are the same, the grammars differ, and the artist's real
    education is learning enough grammars to know which one a given image is speaking.
  </p>

